  "originalQuery": "You are re-architecting a legacy monolithic e-commerce application into a microservices-based architecture. The application uses a single Cloud SQL instance as its backend database. You want to make the database architecture more scalable and suitable for the microservices approach. Your goal is to minimize downtime while implementing changes and ensure that the application remains functional throughout the transition. What should you do?",
  "originalOptions": "A. Gradually introduce microservices while retaining the monolithic database, ensuring all microservices share the same single database instance.<br/>B. Route all database requests through a middleware service. Refactor each microservice to connect to this middleware service instead of directly connecting to the database.<br/>C. Create separate databases for each microservice immediately and update the microservices to use their own database.<br/>D. Use database sharding to split the existing database into multiple shards, with each microservice accessing only its relevant shard.",
